WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.168
-Our first guest tonight is

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:04.270
an Academy Award-
and Tony-nominated

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.671
actor, writer, and director

00:00:05.671 --> 00:00:08.308
you know from films such as
"Training Day" and "Boyhood"

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:11.210
and the shows "The Good Lord
Bird" and "Moon Knight."

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.513
His documentary series
"The Last Movie Stars"

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:14.914
is streaming now on HBO Max.

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:16.249
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:18.551
our good friend Ethan Hawke,
everybody.

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:21.954
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:27.994 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:34.033 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.103
I love it. Looking sharp,
my man. Looking sharp.

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:39.105
-I'm about to go
on the "Louisiana Hayride"

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:40.740
with Hank Williams.
-I love it.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.410
-By the way, that album cover
looks insanely cool.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.312
-Yeah.
-Does it not remind you

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:49.215
a little bit of Khonsu
from "Moon Knight"?

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:50.149
I don't know
if you saw "Moon Knight."

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:51.417
-Yes, I did.
-But it's got

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:53.753
a little Khonsu vibe to it.
-We try very hard --

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.688
we always want to
also promote your work

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:56.856
when we promote
the guest drummer.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.091
-I appreciate that.

00:00:58.091 --> 00:00:59.259
I appreciate you
thinking about me

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:00.993
and I'm thinking about you.

00:01:00.993 --> 00:01:02.462
-Yeah, it's very nice.

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:05.664
Hey, this is -- we've talked
about this, like, two years ago

00:01:05.664 --> 00:01:07.299
how this project came across
your plate.

00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:09.635
This is a wonderful six-part
documentary series

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:11.470
about Paul Newman --
-Can I stop you?

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.172
-Yeah, please.
-This has never happened to me.

00:01:13.172 --> 00:01:17.443
My shirt is stuck on the thing,
and I'm kind of casually

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:19.778
pretending that it's not stuck,
but I got it off now.

00:01:19.778 --> 00:01:21.347
Okay, go on. You were saying?
It was so interesting.

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:23.949
-It would've been so great
to go back and watch

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:25.318
you do the whole interview
where only --

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:28.087
you're only gesticulating
with one hand.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.923
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:32.225
So, Paul Newman
and Joanne Woodward,

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:35.328
this is about their career,
their romance.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.797
It's wonderful.
It's great archival footage.

00:01:37.797 --> 00:01:40.333
And how -- but how did it come
across your plate?

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:43.969
-Well, this wonderful producer
named Emily Wachtel,

00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:47.140
she is really close with the
family -- been a longtime friend

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.507
and felt like it was really time

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:50.443
to make a documentary
about them.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:54.146
They -- I guess
Claire Newman and I

00:01:54.146 --> 00:01:56.682
actually went to the same
high school my senior year,

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:59.485
and so somehow
they thought of me.

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:01.387
I think they thought --
A lot of people

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:02.922
when they think of Paul Newman
and Joanne Woodward,

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:05.391
they have this, like,
"celebrity idea" in their head,

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:09.161
and I think the family really
wanted somebody to revisit them

00:02:09.161 --> 00:02:10.896
as artists and actors

00:02:10.896 --> 00:02:12.331
and as people
who dedicated their life

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:13.866
to the performing arts.

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:15.835
And they thought that I might be
the right person to do that.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.337
I had no idea
what they were thinking about

00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:19.705
or why they did it.

00:02:19.705 --> 00:02:21.774
I mean, I literally was sitting
on the phone thinking,

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:24.777
"That sounds like so much work."
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:27.513
-I desperately
don't want to do this.

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:31.918
But I couldn't get the words
"no" out of my mouth, you know?

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:34.220
So I said, "Sure, yeah.
I'll do it."

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.187
And then there went
three years of my life.

00:02:35.187 --> 00:02:36.656
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:40.392
-Well, the payoff is very nice.
We thank you for your sacrifice.

00:02:40.392 --> 00:02:44.497
-No, I did it because I love
them and I feel like...

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:47.000
Oh, I don't know. You could
talk about it for a long time.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.967
But just the role
of heroes in our life,

00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:50.403
of people
that we can look up to.

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:52.138
These are like
two white people

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:54.406
that were born with a lot
who did a lot with it.

00:02:54.406 --> 00:02:57.710
They dedicated their life to
meaningful, substantive arts

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.945
and they did it with --

00:02:59.945 --> 00:03:01.980
in the middle of having
a 50-year love affair

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.650
and they gave away
hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.020
They're part of -- I just admire
the way they lived their lives.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:10.590
So, whenever you think it's not
possible to be a good person

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.992
and have a good time
and cut a lane for yourself

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:14.760
where you're actually
part of the solution,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.496
it is possible,
and Paul and Joanne are proof.

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:18.865
-Yeah, they really are proof.

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:21.534
And it is really -- You know,
I think that, you know,

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.502
I was certainly aware of, like,
Paul Newman's Own, right?

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:25.138
Like, growing up as a kid,
seeing it at the super market.

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:26.405
I was like, "Oh, look," you
know, "that's,

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:27.807
you know...Butch Cassidy."

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:29.375
But I didn't quite realize
that, you know,

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:31.978
the breadth of their charity.

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:34.414
-Oh, you know, the thing is you
always hear this thing about

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:36.882
this billionaire who gave away
hundreds of millions of dollars,

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:39.519
but Paul and Joanne didn't have
hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:42.755
They gave away exponentially
more money than they kept,

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:44.690
and I think
that's really beautiful.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:48.427
And the fact that they used --
That hadn't been done before,

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:51.930
to create a non-for-profit
company and to use his celebrity

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.935
to generate wealth and
opportunity for other people.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:58.771
-You -- It's called
"The Last Movie Stars,"

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.606
and, you know,
the idea of what's celebrity

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:01.707
and how it's changed

00:04:01.707 --> 00:04:03.009
and how it's harder to,

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:04.510
you know, achieve what they did.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:08.214
But what made you -- You know,
obviously I think this was --

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:10.216
maybe Gore Vidal
had made the observation.

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:13.119
What made you choose it
as the title?

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.021
-I don't know. It caught my --

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:17.790
When I read Gore Vidal said that
they were the last movie stars

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.459
and what he meant was that the
last generation to come up

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:24.263
the same way the first movie
stars did through acting class,

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:28.701
through the studio system,
the hard-earned road

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.270
before -- You know,
back in a period

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.638
where there were a handful
of movies made a year,

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.306
it was a really big deal
to be in a movie.

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:36.642
Now there's, you know,
20 movies come out a weekend,

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:38.344
so it is different.

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:39.412
But when I first read it,
I thought,

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:40.613
"No, they're not movie stars.

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:42.781
They're actors.
Why would he think that?"

00:04:42.781 --> 00:04:45.418
But I found that
it made me think the title,

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:47.320
so I thought,
"Well, that's a good title."

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.955
-Yeah.
-I don't really agree with it,

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:52.525
but I like it.
-I do really love anytime --

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:55.428
I always feel like it's a gift
not just to young actors

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:57.863
but people -- young people
in any field

00:04:57.863 --> 00:04:59.665
who might not
feel sure of themselves.

00:04:59.665 --> 00:05:01.834
Like, in this first episode
especially,

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:04.904
you see a Paul Newman who lacks
confidence and isn't sure.

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:06.272
-That meant the most to me.

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:07.674
I mean, 'cause you can't imagine

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:09.675
Cool Hand Luke being insecure.
-Yeah.

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.210
-I mean,
it just doesn't really --

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.113
But then you start picturing --
Paul Newman was in acting class

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:16.448
with James Dean
and Marlon Brando.

00:05:16.448 --> 00:05:18.284
I mean, those are two of the
other students in the class.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:22.855
Forget, you know, Geraldine
Page, Marilyn Monroe, Ben G--

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.089
I mean, the list is endless.

00:05:24.089 --> 00:05:27.560
So he was not the best student
in that class.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.231
And his life's journey,
which I find so inspiring,

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:35.100
is that he got better and better
and better and better,

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.303
whereas a lot of his peer group
gets burned out,

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:40.506
gets lost, gets confused,
gets crushed by celebrity,

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:41.773
whatever the different stories.

00:05:41.773 --> 00:05:43.643
But he was definitely insecure.

00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:44.843
Would change his shirt
three times a day

00:05:44.843 --> 00:05:46.278
'cause he sweat so much.

00:05:46.278 --> 00:05:49.248
-There's a screen test I guess
with him and James Dean

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:52.050
where he just looks...
He looks like a dork.

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.853
-The actor in you knows,
"Oh, wow, you're nervous."

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:55.053
He doesn't know
how to move his face,

00:05:55.053 --> 00:05:56.622
kind of like me
on this show right now.

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:59.024
[ Laughter ]
And, no, it is.

00:05:59.024 --> 00:06:01.760
I find it so charming to see
him, you know, with James Dean.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.829
-Yeah, because you realize
it's a journey for everybody.

00:06:03.829 --> 00:06:06.866
Nobody's born like the public
version of Paul Newman.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:10.336
No one's born --
-I wish I was, but nobody is.

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:12.305
-Yeah. Alright.
I got a lot more to ask you.

00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:14.374
We'll be right back with more
from Ethan Hawke, everybody.

